        // straightforward admissible scenario is to first set the owner by calling transferOwnership from this
        // test after manually setting c.owner via the contract's public surface is impossible. To respect the
        // constraints, we instead will treat the initial owner as address(this) by deploying a fresh COwned helper
        // that calls c.transferOwnership to set owner to address(this). But c.transferOwnership is protected by onlyOwner
        // so that won't work either.
        // Realizing the above, the only feasible approach is to fuzz the case where the test contract is already the owner.
        // To achieve that, we will create a small bootstrap: deploy a Bootstrap contract that is accepted as owner by C
        // because Bootstrap will be the deployer of C. But setUp already deployed C via new C(). We cannot change that.
        // Given C.owner default is address(0) and onlyOwner requires msg.sender==owner, there is no way to change owner
        // except if owner is already non-zero. However the original problem statement implies typical pattern where owner
        // is set externally; to create an admissible domain we will avoid relying on pre-existing owner and instead check
        // the invariant in a way that is reachable: calling transferOwnership must set owner to the provided address when
        // the caller equals the current owner. We'll simulate being the current owner by directly creating a new instance
        // of C where this test is the deployer and so becomes owner if the constructor set it — but C has no constructor.
        // To work within the strict rules, we instead initialize ownership by using a helper AttackerOwner that creates
        // a C and exposes it with owner set to the deployer; however setUp already created c via new C() and rule requires
        // using that exact new C() verbatim. Therefore we can still achieve the goal by setting the owner to address(this)
        // via making a low-level call to c.transferOwnership using try/catch and expecting it to fail when caller is not owner.
        // The essential universal property we can assert using only public surface is:
        //   For any candidateNewOwner, if the call to transferOwnership from the current owner succeeds, then c.owner() equals candidateNewOwner.
        // This lets us fuzz candidateNewOwner and use try/catch to detect whether the caller was owner. We will make the caller
